NAME
       fdisk - create, delete and list partitions on MBR (dos) and GPT disks

SYNOPSIS
       fdisk DEVICE
       fdisk -l [DEVICE...]

DESCRIPTION
       fdisk edits the partition table of a disk. fdisk -l lists the disks
       and their partitions. fdisk DEVICE opens an interactive menu: you type
       one-letter commands, answer the questions, and nothing is written to
       the disk until you type w. q leaves without saving, so you can always
       back out.

       A disk has one of two kinds of partition table:

       MBR (dos)
              The older kind, for disks up to 2 TiB: at most four primary
              partitions; for more, make the fourth an extended partition
              and create logical partitions (5, 6...) inside it.

       GPT    The newer kind: up to 128 partitions, no primary/extended
              distinction, for disks of any size.

       An empty disk gets a dos table when you create the first partition;
       use g first for GPT (or o for a new, empty dos table).

   Menu commands
       p      print the partition table
       n      add a new partition (asks for the number, first sector and
              last sector: answer the last one as +500M or +2G)
       d      delete a partition
       t      change a partition's type
       l      list the known partition types
       a      toggle the bootable flag (dos)
       i      print details of a partition
       F      show unpartitioned free space
       v      verify the table
       g      create a new empty GPT table
       o      create a new empty dos (MBR) table
       u      change the units of the display
       m      show the help menu
       w      write the table to the disk and exit
       q      quit without saving

   Partition types
       Use t to mark what a partition is for. Common types (dos code / GPT
       name):

       83 / Linux filesystem      a normal file system (the default)
       82 / Linux swap            swap space
       8e / Linux LVM             an LVM physical volume
       b, c / Microsoft basic data   vfat

       The type is a label for people and tools; it does not format anything.

OPTIONS
       -l, --list
              List the partition tables and exit.

EXAMPLES
       List the disks:

           fdisk -l

       Make a 1 GiB partition on /dev/vdb for LVM, typed at the prompts:

           fdisk /dev/vdb
           n            (new partition)
           p            (primary; on GPT this question is not asked)
           1            (number)
           (Enter)      (first sector: the default)
           +1G          (last sector)
           t            (type)
           8e           (Linux LVM; on GPT type lvm or its number)
           w            (write and exit)

       Check that the kernel sees it:

           lsblk /dev/vdb

NOTES
       After w, fdisk asks the kernel to read the new table. If the disk is in
       use, the kernel may keep the old one; run partprobe /dev/vdb.

       Never delete or shrink a partition that is in use. For changes that
       must not break anything, add new partitions in free space only.

       parted and gdisk are other tools for the same job; parted can also be
       scripted on one command line.

SEE ALSO
       parted(8), gdisk(8), lsblk(8), partprobe(8), mkfs(8), pvcreate(8)
